          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which chapter holds high blood pressure, and what does that chapter deliberately exclude?",
          "answer": "High blood pressure is classified in **Chapter 9, diseases of the circulatory system (I00 to I99)**, in the hypertensive-disease block **I10 to I16**. Plain essential (primary) high blood pressure is code **I10**, which needs no further characters. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 9* and *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*, both from the US clinical modification maintained by NCHS and CMS.)\n\nThe chapter does not hold these, and they are coded elsewhere:\n\n- **Transient ischemic attack** (a brief, temporary interruption of blood flow to the brain) is classified with the nervous system, not with circulatory disease.\n- **Congenital malformations** of the heart and large blood vessels belong to the congenital chapter, even in adults.\n- **Circulatory disorders during pregnancy or childbirth** are classified in the obstetric chapter.\n\nHypertension can also move to a different code when it is linked to other conditions. Heart involvement, kidney disease and identifiable underlying causes each have their own combination codes within I11 to I15. Those codes are used together with codes from other chapters, such as the kidney-stage codes in the genitourinary chapter.\n\nThe corpus does not say whether the WHO version of ICD-10 uses the same exact codes. The material here covers the US version only.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
